TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{
  "action": "final",
  "output": {
    "research_concepts": [
      "defensive suppression",
      "halftime adjustment",
      "H2-H1 scoring change",
      "reliability",
      "team trait",
      "counter strategy"
    ],
    "methodological_questions": [
      "How to measure opponents H2-H1 change?",
      "Reliability estimation (split-half, intraclass correlation) for team-season metric",
      "Variance decomposition of defensive effect across halves",
      "Confounding by game script, blowouts, garbage time",
      "Regression to mean when selecting extreme half performances",
      "Adequacy of 120 team-seasons for detecting stable defensive trait",
      "Isolating defensive from offensive influence on opponent scoring change"
    ],
    "historical_evidence": [],
    "failed_approaches": [],
    "evidence_establishes": [],
    "evidence_does_not_establish": [
      "No prior research on this specific question was available in the retrieved records, so nothing has been established or ruled out by earlier project work."
    ],
    "own_inferences": [
      "The quantity 'opponent H2-H1 change' can be derived from play-by-play data, and its stability across games and seasons can be estimated via intraclass correlation or hierarchical modeling.",
      "The primary challenges are: (1) the change score inherits regression-to-the-mean artifacts because first-half opponent scoring is a noisy baseline; (2) second-half scoring is confounded by game script (blowouts, garbage time, opponent resting starters); (3) with only 30 teams observed over 4 seasons, power to detect a modest team-level variance component is limited.",
      "Without controlling for first-half performance and game context, any apparent reliability in raw H2-H1 differences would be suspect."
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The true size of a defensive halftime-adjustment trait, if it exists, is unknown; it may be so small that the available sample cannot distinguish it from noise.",
      "How much of the second-half scoring change is attributable to the opponent's own offensive quality versus the defense's adjustment is difficult to isolate observationally."
    ],
    "historical_effect": {
      "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
      "explanation": "No historical evidence was retrieved; the decision rests solely on the methodological assessment of the question with the available data."
    },
    "testability": {
      "p_testable": 0.65,
      "blocking_constraints": [
        {
          "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
          "text": "Only 30 teams over 4 seasons; detecting a small between-team variance in halftime-adjustment effect likely requires a larger number of independent team observations or longer time series."
        },
        {
          "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
          "text": "The H2-H1 change score is mechanically correlated with the first-half opponent scoring baseline, creating regression-to-the-mean artifacts unless the baseline is properly conditioned on."
        },
        {
          "code": "CONFOUNDING",
          "text": "Game script (point differential at halftime, garbage time, opponent resting patterns) strongly influences second-half scoring, potentially swamping the defensive signal."
        }
      ]
    },
    "prior_work": {
      "already_answered": "unknown",
      "cited": [],
      "summary": "No earlier project studies on this exact question were found; the question remains open."
    },
    "risks": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "With 30 teams and 4 seasons, power to detect a small intraclass correlation for a defensive-adjustment trait may be low."
      },
      {
        "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
        "text": "The raw H2-H1 opponent scoring difference is negatively correlated with first-half opponent scoring, a classic regression-to-the-mean pattern that can create spurious stability if not modeled."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Second-half opponent scoring is heavily influenced by game situation (blowouts, garbage time, opponent tactical shifts) that are not purely a function of the defense."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Per-game opponent half-to-half scoring changes are noisy, and aggregating over only a season per team may yield an unreliable metric, attenuating any between-team correlation."
      },
      {
        "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
        "text": "When a defense already holds an opponent to very low scoring in the first half, the scoring change may be bounded on the low side (floor effect), distorting the distribution."
      }
    ],
    "predicted_outcome": {
      "supported": 0.30,
      "rejected": 0.40,
      "untestable": 0.10,
      "artifact": 0.15,
      "uncertain": 0.05
    },
    "decision": "proceed_with_changes",
    "design": {
      "estimand": "The team-specific, season-level causal effect of a defense on opponent second-half points per possession (PPP) relative to first-half PPP, after controlling for opponent offensive strength and game script; the stability (intraclass correlation, ICC) of this effect across half-season splits and across seasons.",
      "unit": "Defensive team-season, with within-season half-splits for reliability estimation; game-level data is used to estimate the team-season adjustment parameter.",
      "sample": "All regular-season games from the 2022-23 through 2025-26 seasons. Garbage-time minutes (defined as segments where score differential exceeds 20 points and quarter ≥ 4) are excluded. Fourth quarters of games with a point differential > 20 at the start of the quarter are also removed. Only games with both teams having at least 20 possessions in each half after garbage-time removal are retained.",
      "power": "Power will be assessed by simulation: using a hierarchical model with plausible effect sizes (e.g., team-level standard deviation in adjustment effect of 0.02–0.05 PPP) and the observed number of games per team-season, determine the minimum detectable ICC with 80% power at alpha = 0.05. If power is inadequate, at least three full seasons (2023-24 onward) will be used to extend the time series.",
      "controls": "Fixed effects: opponent season-long offensive rating (ORtg), home/away indicator, rest advantage (back-to-back indicator for both teams), point differential at halftime, half-level pace (estimated possessions), an indicator for whether the opponent's star players are available (derived from rotation data). The defense team-season random intercept will be the primary quantity.",
      "falsification": "1. Compute the same H2-H1 defensive trait for offenses (team's own scoring change) and test whether it shows similar reliability; if it does, the trait may reflect general game-script artifacts rather than pure defense. 2. Shuffle games across teams within season and re-estimate ICC; reliability should vanish. 3. Split the season into first half and second half (by game date) and compare—large discrepancy would indicate non-stationarity (coaching changes, trades). 4. Check whether the team random effect is stable when using only first-half opponent PPP as outcome; it should be, but the adjustment effect should be orthogonal.",
      "multiplicity": "Only one primary hypothesis (ICC > 0) will be tested; any sensitivity analyses using alternative specifications will be labeled exploratory and corrected with Bonferroni.",
      "decision_rule": "A Bayesian hierarchical model (team random intercepts with partial pooling) will be fit. The hypothesis is supported if the 95% credible interval for the intraclass correlation (posterior of σ_team^2 / (σ_team^2 + σ_resid^2)) excludes zero and the posterior probability of σ_team > 0 exceeds 0.975. The study will conclude 'rejected' if the 90% credible interval for ICC is entirely below a pre-specified smallest effect size of interest (e.g., ICC = 0.05) and the posterior median is near zero.",
      "data_version": "NBA 2022-23 through 2025-26 regular season play-by-play, substitution, and matchup data as available on 2026-10-06."
    }
  }
}

